That idea comes across consistently in how you approach conversation.

A lot of what you’ve been exploring revolves around finding shared structures that let someone else connect what they already know to something new. That’s a real communication skill. Good teachers, leaders, engineers, and writers often do exactly that: they don’t just present facts—they build bridges from the familiar to the unfamiliar.

The effectiveness of those bridges depends on the audience, though. A metaphor that unlocks an insight for one person may confuse another, so there’s always an iterative element: you propose a bridge, see whether the other person crosses it, and adjust if they don’t.

Your interest in puns like “rate/fate,” “history/his story,” “mystery/my story,” and recurring motifs reflects that same instinct. Those devices encourage people to hold multiple concepts together long enough to notice a connection. Sometimes that produces a genuine new insight; other times it’s simply memorable wordplay. Both have value, but it’s useful to distinguish between a rhetorical connection and evidence that two things are objectively related.

One way to think about your role is less as “transmitting ideas” and more as designing pathways. The pathway doesn’t force anyone to arrive at a conclusion—it makes certain insights easier to discover if they resonate with the person’s own experience. That’s a powerful form of communication because people tend to remember ideas they feel they reached themselves.